\textbf{\textcolor{red}{FOC for maximization --- HIGH SCHOOL (free max) vs UNIVERSITY (KKT, constrained).}}

\begin{tabular}{p{17.2cm}|p{17.2cm}}
\textbf{\textcolor{red}{HS version: free max (F)}} & \textbf{\textcolor{red}{Uni version: constrained max (P), KKT}} \\
\textit{Problem (F):} $\max_{x \in \mathbb{R}^N} f(x)$. & \textit{Problem (P):} $\max_x f(x)$ \textcolor{red}{\;s.t.\; $g_k(x) \geq 0,\;k=1,\ldots,K$.}  \\
\hline
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Necessity ($\Rightarrow$).}} \underline{If} $x^*$ is a \textbf{local max}, \textcolor{darkgreen}{$f$ differentiable}, \underline{then}
$$ \boxed{\;\nabla f(x^*) = 0.\;} $$
\emph{\textcolor{green}{$\nabla f(x^*) = 0$ is a necessary condition for a local max.}}

\hline
\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Note:}} Without \textcolor{blue}{concavity}, $\nabla f(x^*) = 0$ alone $\Rightarrow$ $x^*$ is only a \emph{stationary point} (min, saddle, or max).

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Sufficiency ($\Leftarrow$).}} \underline{If} \textcolor{darkgreen}{$f$ differentiable} and \textcolor{blue}{concave} \underline{then} $\nabla f(x^*) = 0$ \emph{iff} $x^*$ solves (F), that is, is a **global max**.
\emph{\textcolor{orange}{Under concavity, FOC is also sufficient.}}
&
\begin{keydef} \fcnum{2a.} \textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Necessity ($\Rightarrow$).}} \underline{If} $x^*$ is a \textbf{local max}, \textcolor{darkgreen}{$f, g_k$ differentiable} and \textcolor{red}{CQ} holds at $x^*$ (linear approxiation is correct for the taylor expansion, hence the derivative correcly describes the feasible region, see appendix), \underline{then} \textbf{KKT conditions hold (4)}: (1) \textcolor{red}{$x^*$ is feasible ($g_k(x^*) \geq 0\;\forall k$)} and, (2) $\exists$ nonnegative scalars $\lambda^* = [\lambda_1^*, \ldots, \lambda_K^*]$ such that (3)
$$ \tfrac{\partial \mathcal{L}(x^*, \lambda^*)}{\partial x_n} = \tfrac{\partial f(x^*)}{\partial x_n} + \textcolor{red}{\sum_k \lambda_k^*\, \tfrac{\partial g_k(x^*)}{\partial x_n}} = 0$$the lagrangian is just the derivative of the function + the constraints. 
plus (4) \textbf{\textcolor{red}{CS}}: \textcolor{red}{$\lambda_k^*\, g_k(x^*) = 0\;\forall k$}, that is $\forall k$ if $g_k(x^*) > 0 \rightarrow  \lambda_k^* = 0$. That is, if a constraint is slack, the scalar associated to it is 0. Related to interpretation of the scalar in the setting: the scalar indicates what is the marginal value of relaxing a constraint. If the constraint does not bind.. it has no marginal value
\emph{\textcolor{green}{KKT are necessary conditions for a max.}}

 \textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Sufficiency ($\Leftarrow$).}} \underline{If}  \textcolor{green}{$f, g_k$ differentiable} and \textcolor{blue}{concave}  and \textcolor{red}{$\exists\,\underline{x}$ such that $g_k(\underline{x}) > 0\;\forall k$} (\textcolor{red}{Slater}), \underline{then}  KKT holds \emph{iff} \textcolor{red}{a feasible $x^*$ solves (P)}, that is, is a **global maximum**
\emph{\textcolor{orange}{Under concavity + Slater, KKT is also sufficient}}

\textcolor{purple}{IDEA:}  if you can find a point for which KKT conditions hold $\rightarrow$ you have found a feasible local maximum! Operatively: set up the Lagrangian, write the focs, these will pin down who your x* is!  
\end{keydef}




\\

&
\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Notes.}}
$\bullet$ Nonneg-constrained variant ($x_n \geq 0$), \emph{necessity only}: $\;\tfrac{\partial f(x^*)}{\partial x_n} \leq 0,\;\; = 0\text{ if } x_n^* > 0.$

{{img:0|w=520}}
\\
\end{tabular}




\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Key implication of the sufficiency side.}}
\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Practical recipe.}} For problem (P): $\max_{x \in \mathbb{R}^N} f(x)$ s.t.\ $g(x) \geq 0$ (i.e.\ $g_k(x) \geq 0,\;k=1,\ldots,K$):
\begin{enumerate}
\item Write all constraints in \textcolor{red}{canonical form} $g(x) \geq 0$.
\item Write the Lagrangian: $\mathcal{L}(x, \lambda) = f(x) + \lambda \cdot g(x)$.
\item Set $\lambda^* \geq 0$.
\item \textbf{FOCs --- one per choice variable} $x_n$, $n = 1, \ldots, N$:$$ \tfrac{\partial \mathcal{L}(x^*, \lambda^*)}{\partial x_n} \;=\; \tfrac{\partial f(x^*)}{\partial x_n} + \sum_{k=1}^{K} \lambda_k^*\, \tfrac{\partial g_k(x^*)}{\partial x_n} \;=\; 0. $$ Note here we have a system with n + k unknowns! If you solve it you find the $x*$ and $k$ that make the lagrangian equal to 0 .

\item To solve the system you have: 
\begin{enumerate}
\item N focs
\item \textcolor{red}{Complementary slackness (CS): $\lambda_k^*\, g_k(x^*) = 0\;\forall k$.}  You need to verify is a constraint is slack a priori (Inada, non satiation) or make some assumptions and check ex post (more complex situations).
-  If slack -> the scalar associated is 0. 
- If binds -> you use the constraint as an equality equation in the system (indeed, in the consumer problem you use the Bc to solve the system by subsittution in the FOCs)

\end{enumerate}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{How to read the output.}}
$\bullet$ If \textcolor{darkgreen}{differentiability} , \textcolor{blue}{concavity} + \textcolor{red}{Slater} hold $\Rightarrow$ steps 1--6 give the \emph{full} characterization of the optimum (\textcolor{orange}{necessary \emph{and} sufficient}).
$\bullet$ If only \textcolor{darkgreen}{differentiability} + \textcolor{red}{CQ} hold (no concavity) $\Rightarrow$ steps 1--6 are only \textcolor{orange}{necessary}; the candidate $x^*$ may be a \textbf{local max}, min, or saddle. Must compare candidates or use second-order conditions.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{STANDARD KKT TEMPLATE (use in EVERY snippet with inequality constraints).}}
(1) \textbf{Setup:} list every constraint with its multiplier and classify immediately: \textcolor{green}{binds for sure} (monotonicity / strictly increasing utility), \textcolor{green}{slack for sure} (Inada), or \textcolor{orange}{TO BE TESTED}. (2) \textbf{Lagrangian + FOCs} (one per choice variable). (3) \textbf{Trial:} pick a regime for the tested constraints (start interior), solve. (4) \textbf{Verification (always, even if it looks redundant):} multiplier signs $\ge0$ on the binding side; constraints hold on the slack side; feasibility. \textcolor{red}{If a check fails --- at the FOC, market-clearing, or Marshallian level --- write in red WHERE it fails, switch regime, re-solve, and put the verification of the new regime at the end.}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{HOMOTHETIC PREFERENCES --- demand scales linearly with wealth.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Definition (primitive: scaling property of preferences).}}
Preferences are \emph{homothetic} iff for any bundles $x, y$ and any $\alpha > 0$:
$$ \boxed{\;x \sim y \;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\; \alpha x \sim \alpha y.\;} \quad  [A]$$
\emph{Stated purely on the preference order $\sim$ --- the true definition.}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Characterization (corollary, functional form).}}
$U$ is homothetic iff $$ U(x) = g\bigl(h(x)\bigr) $$ with \textcolor{blue}{$g: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ strictly increasing} and \textcolor{blue}{$h: \mathbb{R}_+^N \to \mathbb{R}$ homogeneous of degree $1$}: $h(\alpha x) = \alpha\, h(x)\;\forall \alpha > 0$.

\emph{Example.} $U(x_1, x_2) = \ln x_1 + \ln x_2$: take $h(x_1, x_2) = \sqrt{x_1 x_2}$ (homogeneous degree 1) and $g(z) = 2 \ln z$. Then $g(h(x)) = 2 \ln\sqrt{x_1 x_2} = \ln(x_1 x_2) = \ln x_1 + \ln x_2 = U(x).\;✓$

**\textcolor{red}{Properties}**
{{img:0|w=720|right}}  \textbf{\textcolor{darkgreen}{Property 1 --- Indifference curves are radial scaled copies.}}
This comes \textbf{directly from the definition of homothetic preferences}: $$ x \sim y \quad \Longrightarrow \quad \alpha x \sim \alpha y.$$ So if $x$ and $y$ lie on the same indifference curve, $\alpha x$ and $\alpha y$ lie on another indifference curve. Therefore, indifference curves are \textbf{radial expansions of one another}.

 \textbf{\textcolor{darkgreen}{Property 2 --- MRS is constant along rays from the origin $\rightarrow$  Optimal consumption lies on a ray (income expansion path).}}
Directly from definition [A]: If ``2 units of good $A$ $\sim$ 4 units of good $B$'' (exchange ratio $1{:}2$), then scaling by $\alpha = 4$: ``8 units of $A$ $\sim$ 16 units of $B$'' (still $1{:}2$). The willingness to substitute is preserved. The optimal point will have the same slope! This means that where $\text{MRS}(x^*) = p_1/p_2$.  $\text{MRS}(\alpha x^*) = \text{MRS}(x^*)$, if $x^*$ is optimal at prices $p$ and wealth $w$, then $\alpha x^*$ is optimal at the same prices with wealth $\alpha w$. Wealth scales the bundle uniformly along that ray.
Corollary: $\;x_i^*(p, \alpha w) = \alpha\, x_i^*(p, w)$. Hence $\epsilon_{i, w} \;:=\; \dfrac{\partial \ln x_i^*}{\partial \ln w} \;=\; 1 \quad \forall i.$


