\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Why is the aggregate endowment ratio equal to the optimal individual consumption ratio?}}

\emph{The point:} agents are identical and homothetic, so each one wants a fixed consumption ratio $x_a^h/x_b^h$ (wealth-independent, and the \emph{same} across agents). The only feasible value of that common ratio --- i.e., **the only one for which prices exist** that clear the market --- is the one matching the aggregate endowment ratio $\omega_a/\omega_b$. Note how prices reflect scarcity!

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{QUASILINEAR PREFERENCES --- no wealth effect on non-numeraire goods.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Definition.}}
$$ \boxed{\;U(x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_N) = \alpha\, x_1 + u(x_2, \ldots, x_N), \quad \alpha > 0.\;} $$
Good 1 is the \textcolor{orange}{numeraire}: utility \emph{linear} in $x_1$, so $MU_1 = \alpha$ is \textbf{constant} (no diminishing returns). $x_1$ unrestricted in sign (``money'', will see why).

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{FACT 1 ---Numeraire absorbs residual wealth }}
{{img:1|w=1100|right}}
Again the plot already described:
- Once you buy enough of the other good and reach the floor, you stop buying it and buy only the numeraire good. \textbf{Note:} Same $MB/p$ across agents does \textbf{not} mean same $x_1$ (numeraire);  Consequence:  $x_1^*(p, w) = w - \sum_{i\geq 2} p_i\, x_i^*(p)$. Numeraire absorbs all residual wealth. So:  $x_2^*$ for $i \geq 2$ \textcolor{orange}{independent of $w$} $\Rightarrow$ wealth elasticity $\epsilon_{i,w} = 0$ for non-numeraire goods.
- If agents have same preferences on the other goods: it means same they will consume the same $x_2^*$ (non-numeraire, since $\partial u/\partial x_2 = \alpha p_2$ pins a common $x_2^*$ when preferences are identical). All wealth differences go into $x_1$. 
Note how the marshallian for $x_2^*$ is independent of wealth, hence, as long as all agents are interior, there is no change in AD and hence no change in prices generated by changes in endowments  

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{FACT 2 --- Geometric reading.}}
Indifference curves are \textcolor{orange}{horizontal translates} of one another. Consider IC at different w: 1) Same MRS = Same slope. 2) more of good $x_1$ so horizontal shift 
