\textbf{\textcolor{red}{REAL ENDOWMENTS --- when wealth itself depends on prices.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Setup: nominal vs.\ real wealth.}}

*Usually*: $x_i^*(p, w)$, with $w$ exogenous.

\emph{Real-endowment problem}: Their wealth is then \emph{endogenously determined} by the value of what they own: $w(p) \;=\; p \cdot \omega  $. Hence: $ \hat x_i^*(p, \omega) \;=\; x_i^*\bigl(p,\; p \cdot \omega\bigr). $

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Step 1 --- The chain rule: two channels for a price change.}}

When $p_j$ rises, the consumer feels it \emph{twice}, by the chain rule: $$ \boxed{\;\dfrac{\partial \hat x_i^*(p, \omega)}{\partial p_j} \;=\; \underbrace{\dfrac{\partial x_i^*(p, w)}{\partial p_j}\bigg|_{w\text{ fixed}}}_{\text{Marshallian price effect}} \;+\; \underbrace{\dfrac{\partial x_i^*(p, w)}{\partial w} \cdot \dfrac{\partial w}{\partial p_j}}_{\text{endowment-wealth effect}}.\;} $$
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Step 2 --- The first term IS the standard Slutsky decomposition.}}

The Slutsky equation splits it:
$$ \boxed{\;\dfrac{\partial x_i^*(p, w)}{\partial p_j}\bigg|_{w} \;=\; \underbrace{\dfrac{\partial x_i^h(p, \bar U)}{\partial p_j}}_{\text{substitution } S_{ij}} \;-\; \underbrace{x_j^*(p, w) \cdot \dfrac{\partial x_i^*}{\partial w}}_{\text{(Marshallian) income effect}}.\;} $$


\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Preamble.}}
$\bullet$ Different points on the same Indifference Curve represent the loci with same utility under different prices, hence, requiring different w to be attained
$\bullet$ "Bad" price systems (or "stupid" utility functions) can be very inefficient — they require a lot of wealth to reach a given indifference curve.

