\textbf{\textcolor{red}{CONTRACT CURVE --- the subset of PS that's mutually-improving over $\omega$.}}

{{img:0|w=900|right}}
 \emph{Plain English.} The Contract Curve is the set of allocations that are both \textbf{Pareto efficient} AND make \textbf{==both agents (**they both have to accept!!!**)== at least as well off as at their endowment $\omega$}.  

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{(1) Definition.}}
$$ \boxed{\;\mathrm{CC}(\omega) \;=\; \bigl\{x \in \mathrm{PS} \;:\; U^i(x^i) \,\geq\, U^i(\omega^i) \;\;\forall i\bigr\}.\;} $$
Two ingredients:
$\bullet$ \emph{Efficiency}: $x \in \mathrm{PS}$ (cannot improve everyone).
$\bullet$ \emph{Individual rationality}: each agent weakly prefers $x$ to $\omega$ (voluntary participation).

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{COMPETITIVE EQUILIBRIUM (CE) --- prices and allocation where markets clear and consumers optimize.}}



\begin{keydef} \fcnum{4a.}
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Definition.}} A pair $(\hat p, \hat x)$ with $\hat p \in \mathbb{R}^N_+$ and $\hat x = (\hat x^1, \ldots, \hat x^I)$ such that:

\textbf{1) Consumer optimization:} for every agent $i$,
$$ \boxed{\;\hat x^i \;\in\; \arg\max_{x^i \geq 0} U^i(x^i) \;\;\text{s.t.}\;\; \hat p \cdot x^i \leq \hat p \cdot \omega^i.\;} 
$$
\emph{(Each agent's subproblem follows the standard template --- choice variables $x^i=(x^i_1,\dots,x^i_N)$; constraints: budget $\hat p\cdot x^i\le\hat p\cdot\omega^i$ $(\lambda^i)$ and non-negativity $x^i\ge0$ $(\mu^i)$; \textcolor{green}{binding status: budget binds for sure (monotonicity), $x^i\ge0$ slack for sure under Inada, otherwise TESTED ex post;} Lagrangian $\mathcal{L}^i=U^i(x^i)-\lambda^i(\hat p\cdot x^i-\hat p\cdot\omega^i)+\sum_n\mu^i_n x^i_n$; FOCs $\Rightarrow$ Marshallian $x^i(\hat p,\hat p\cdot\omega^i)$ --- see the consumer-maximization guide.)}
\textbf{2) Market clearing:} for every good $n$,
$$ \boxed{\;\sum_{i=1}^I \hat x_n^i \;=\; \sum_{i=1}^I \omega_n^i \;=\; \omega_n.\;} $$

==\textcolor{orange}{Note the CE must be studied given Omega. }==
\end{keydef}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{PARETO OPTIMUM --- UPF and PS, the two coordinate views.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{(1) Utility Space U.}}
$$ \mathcal{U} \;=\; \bigl\{(u^1, u^2) \;:\; \exists\, x \in \mathcal{F},\; U^i(x^i) \geq u^i \;\forall i\bigr\} $$
is the set of \emph{utility profiles dominated by some feasible allocation}. Elegant definition!

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{(2) UPF --- definition (no domination).}}
$(\bar u^1, \bar u^2) \in \mathcal{U}$ is on the UPF iff
$$ \boxed{\;\nexists\, (\tilde u^1, \tilde u^2) \in \mathcal{U} : \tilde u^i \geq \bar u^i \;\forall i,\; > \text{some } j.\;} $$
set of utiliteis that are undominated.
\textcolor{purple}{Note that this DOES NOT MEAN AT ALL that the aggregate utility is maximum! (if utilities are concave, equal split should maximize them). This is just saying that there is no way to make an agent better of without makign another worse off! You see this in the fact that you do not want any element of the couple to be dominated. So say all agents except 1 are better off, then such allocaiton is not PO}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{(3) PS --- definition (no domination).}}
$\bar x \in \mathcal{F}$ is Pareto Optimal iff
$$ \boxed{\;\nexists\, y \in \mathcal{F} : U^i(y^i) \geq U^i(\bar x^i) \;\forall i,\; > \text{some } j.\;} $$
and $\mathrm{PS} = \{x \in \mathcal{F} : x \text{ is PO}\}$. Basically is the allocaiton associated to the utilities that cannot be dominated!

