\textbf{4) Feasibility}
\emph{Feasibility}: all points in the Edgeworth box are feasible (no resource is wasted; total endowment is conserved). This is because the axes represent total resources at most. 

\textbf{5) CE and PO}
SWT: the PO is where the indifference curves are tangent. SO if we have a CE it is a PO. But we may have some PO for which no CE exists (that are not supportable by any system of prices)

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{MODELLING TAXATION (HSV FRAMEWORK).}}

Gov spends each year  G (defense, healthcare...)
\[ G = \int T(y)\, df(y) \;\;\;\;\textbf{[A]} \quad\quad\text{Totally diff than before.} \]\[ T(y) = y - c, \quad \text{where} \;\; \boxed{\;c \;=\; B\, y^{1-\tau},\;\; B > 0,\;\; \tau \in [0, 1].\;} \]
\emph{So planner chooses $B, \tau$ to satisfy [A]. Note: if you choose $\tau$, $B$ needs to adjust to have G.}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Reading $\tau$ (progressivity dial).}}
$\bullet$ \emph{If $\tau = 0$} (0 progressivity), tax is indep.\ of income: $c = B y$ (flat tax, \% of $y$).
$\bullet$ \emph{If $\tau > 0$}, $c < y$ $\to$ \textbf{progressive tax} (rich pay an higher %) 
$\bullet$ \emph{$\tau = 1$}: total tax, $c = B$, \emph{we all consume the same amount} (communism). Hyper progressivity: the rich pays so much he gets to the poor level of consumption.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Appendix --- Constraint Qualifications (CQ).}}



KKT captures only the \textbf{linear} component of the Taylor expansion of $g_k$ around $x^*$ (the effect of a small step $d$ through $g_k(x^*+d) \approx g_k(x^*) + \nabla g_k(x^*)^\top d$). The \textbf{non-linear} (curvature) component is invisible to the KKT conditions. When curvature dominates near $x^*$ --- as in Constraint B, where $g''(0) = -2$ carries all the local information --- the linear picture is uninformative and KKT can fail even at a genuine optimum. A CQ is precisely the condition that rules out this pathology.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Two constraints, same feasible point $x^* = 0$, opposite verdicts.}}

\textbf{Constraint A.}\quad $g(x) = -x$, so the constraint $g(x) \geq 0$ reads $x \leq 0$. Gradient at $0$: $g'(0) = -1 \neq 0$.
$\bullet$ Move left ($x < 0$): $g(x) > 0$ --- \emph{feasible}.
$\bullet$ Move right ($x > 0$): $g(x) < 0$ --- \emph{infeasible}.
The gradient tells you \emph{exactly} which side is allowed. The linear picture matches the truth. \textbf{\textcolor{red}{CQ holds.}}

\textbf{Constraint B.}\quad $g(x) = -x^2$, so the constraint $g(x) \geq 0$ reads $x^2 \leq 0$. Gradient at $0$: $g'(0) = 0$.
$\bullet$ Linear picture: $g'(0) \cdot d = 0 \geq 0$ for \emph{any} direction $d$, so KKT thinks every step is allowed.
$\bullet$ Truth: $(-0.001)^2 > 0$ and $(0.001)^2 > 0$ --- \emph{no} step is feasible. The only feasible point is $x = 0$ itself.
The linear picture lies. \textbf{\textcolor{red}{CQ fails.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Where does the failure come from?}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Preamble: why a single-price monopoly is inefficient, and the road to adverse selection.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{1. Social POV --- what SHOULD be produced.}}
{{img:3|w=420|right}} Two agents (generalizable to $n$), each with a CONTINUOUS demand, one lying above the other: $d_1$ sophisticated, $d_2$ frugal. The market demand $D$ is their aggregate:

$D$ is the \textbf{HORIZONTAL sum} of $d_1,d_2$ (private good: each unit is consumed by ONE agent --- \textcolor{orange}{summing the curves vertically is public-goods math, Samuelson}). Produce while $MSB = P > MC$ ON $D$: produce until $P = 4$, i.e.\ $Q^*=4$.

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{2. The problem has TWO LAYERS.}} Let the two agents now have CONTINUOUS demands (no longer 0--1): agent 1 sophisticated, agent 2 frugal.
$\bullet$ \textbf{Layer 1 (ACROSS agents):} selling at the frugal's $MB=MC$ maximizes CS and welfare, but firm profit is $0$; one price cannot fit both --- the firm sells a jacket agent 1 would pay $1000$ for at the margin, at the $500$ agent 2 wants (say $MC=500$).
$\bullet$ \textbf{Layer 2 (WITHIN agent):} agent 1 buys 3 jackets at $1000$ each, but he would have paid $9000$ for the first, $5000$ for the second, $3000$ for the third --- a uniform per-unit price leaves all that inframarginal surplus unpriced.

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{3. Firm POV --- maximize profit.}}
\begin{enumerate}
\item IF the firm cannot differentiate prices across consumers, selling at the shared price of $4$ is not profit maximizing --- profit there is $0$! Somebody would be willing to pay $9$ but can buy at $4$ (consumer surplus is generated!).
\item The firm's optimality condition is $MR(Q)=MC(Q)$, with $MR=\Delta TR$ (total revenue). Note $MR(Q)<P$. Truly the problem is deeper: with the two multi-unit demands aggregated into $D$, the company asks: if I produce 2 units, how much do they pay on average? If 3 units? --- it is not one agent paying one price and stop. See table:
\begin{tabular}{p{9.5cm}|p{9.5cm}|p{9.5cm}|p{9.5cm}}
\textbf{P} & \textbf{Q  (from $P = 10 - Q$)} & \textbf{MSB  (value of the unit)} & \textbf{MR  ($\Delta$TR)} \\
\hline
9 & 1 & 9 & 9 \\
\hline
8 & 2 & 8 & 7 \\
\hline
7 & 3 & 7 & 5 \\
\hline
6 & 4 & 6 & 3 \\
\hline
5 & 5 & 5 & 1 \\
\hline
4 & 6 & 4 & $-1$ \\
\end{tabular}
\item SOLUTION 1 (uniform price): {{img:1|w=280|right}} Sell at $MR = MC$. The firm captures some PS (see the graph: \textcolor{red}{PS}, \textcolor{green}{CS}, \textbf{DWL} in black) and CS is smaller. At $Q^M$, units with $MSB > MC$ are \emph{not produced}: a buyer willing to pay 1,100 for a jacket that costs 900 to make --- a socially valuable trade --- is refused, because executing it requires dropping the price for the buyers who would have paid 10,000. \textbf{That is the DWL.}
\item SOLUTION 2 (perfect price discrimination, in words): discriminate \textbf{ACROSS and WITHIN agents} --- walk down each agent's OWN demand charging each unit its marginal value (equivalently: personalized two-part tariffs, fee = surplus, marginal price =MC). The firm extracts ALL surplus: CS =0, every unit with $MB>MC$ trades, \textbf{no DWL}.
\end{enumerate}




\textbf{\textcolor{red}{New setting: LINEAR utility (flat MB) and INCREASING MC.}}
{{img:4|w=760|right}}
With linear utility the demand lines are FLAT (MB$_i=\theta_i$): each agent values every unit the same. Left: one uniform price $\Rightarrow$ \textcolor{green}{CS}, \textcolor{red}{PS}, \textbf{DWL} (black). Right: discriminate ACROSS types ($p_i=\theta_i$) $\Rightarrow$ the firm extracts everything, DWL $=0$.
\textcolor{orange}{Note: here you only need discrimination ACROSS types, NOT within --- with a flat MB every unit is worth the same to the agent, so there is NO within-agent inframarginal surplus to extract.}


NO MATTER IF YOU CALL QUALITY OR QUANTITY THE X AXIS 

Now, if you want a quality / qunaitty optimizaiotn the problem gets one more dimension. But you can frame the above as a problem of quality, discarding the quantity component. You can fix the quantity at whatever number (1, 2... it is not pinned down by the system) and let the D and MC  evolve just wrt the quality (truly, if you want to complicat ethism you should gaev the D, MR and Mc as funciton of two params: quality and quantity). At the moment MR and MC depends just of quality, quantity becomes undetermined, fix it at 1. 


Now, with the focus on quality, we can discuss the menu problem: on airplanes you have business flyers (high WTP, firm account) and leisure flyers (low WTP, out of pocket); right now: one cabin, one fare. You would like perfect price discrimination, but with $n$ types you would need to KNOW who is what. Say 2 types (B and L): if you can observe both, you perfectly price discriminate. You cannot. \textbf{Engineer a menu}: business class or economy --- let them self-select.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{5. Aside: is the rich's WTP really higher at \emph{every} quality?}}

The textbook screening logic assumes WTP$_H(q) > $ WTP$_L(q)$ for every $q$ (linear, single-crossing). Real life is messier.

\emph{Hotel example.} If you're used to 5-star hotels, a 3-star feels \emph{bad} --- barely an upgrade from staying home, almost a disutility. For someone whose default is staying home, a 3-star vacation is \emph{great}. Similarly, a poor consumer may think a \$10k jacket is crazy: they value it at \$1k, same as the \$1k jacket; the rest is wasted money.

Both WTPs are S-shaped (sigmoid); the poor's plateau hits earlier and lower:

{{img:2|w=560}}

The two curves \textbf{cross}: at low quality, poor's WTP $>$ rich's WTP; at high quality, rich's WTP $\gg$ poor's WTP.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Why this matters.}} In this realistic case the IC problem becomes \emph{easier}, not harder. The rich is \textbf{already disincentivised} from the low-quality bundle, because they get \emph{little utility} from it. Screening works \textbf{more cleanly}: less degradation needed, less rent paid.

\textcolor{purple}{\emph{Reading: the linear-WTP / single-crossing case is the \textbf{worst} scenario for the firm --- the rich is maximally tempted by every cheaper bundle. Any non-linearity (saturation, taste differences, sigmoidal preferences) makes screening strictly easier. Our analysis is the binding case.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Key takeaway.}} The screening contract is the firm's \emph{best response} when perfect PD is impossible. It is \emph{strictly better} than ``one price for everyone'' (the naive monopoly outcome) and \emph{strictly worse} than first-best (which would require observable types). This second-best, with its IC + IR constraints, is the workhorse of the adverse-selection chapter --- the rest of the analysis just works out the optimal menu.

